The three forms, executed:

```go
// 1. Simple
ints := []int{3, 1, 2}
sort.Ints(ints)             // [1, 2, 3]

// 2. sort.Slice — any slice, any comparison
type User struct { Name string; Age int }
users := []User{{"Al", 30}, {"Bo", 25}}
sort.Slice(users, func(i, j int) bool {
    return users[i].Age < users[j].Age  // by Age
})

// 3. sort.Sort — for types implementing sort.Interface (Len, Less, Swap)
// (the old pattern; sort.Slice replaced it for most cases)
```

The less function: sort.Slice's second argument answers 'is element i less than element j?' — the sort uses your comparison to order. The sort's stability: sort.Slice is NOT stable (equal elements can be reordered) — sort.SliceStable is the stable variant (when equal elements must keep their relative order).

The Go 1.21+ slices package (the future):
```go
import "slices"
slices.Sort(users)  // by a natural ordering (Ordered constraint)
slices.SortFunc(users, func(a, b User) int { return a.Age - b.Age })  // by comparison
```

The complexity: O(n log n), in-place (the slice's memory is rearranged, not copied).

The reverse: sort.Slice with the flipped comparison, or sort.Reverse(...) for the interface form.
